# Unreliable memory
#
# `recall` does not return true or false, and it is not free.
#
# A memory carries four stances -- affirm, deny, doubt, hope -- which accumulate
# separately and are never reconciled behind your back.  Aiming an emotion at a
# claim rather than at a person is how you take a position on it: doubt and hope
# are their own stances, and feeling anything else about something is a way of
# believing it happened.  Confidence is read off the balance, so a claim can be
# believed and denied at once, and the contradictions it holds are part of the
# answer.
#
# The interesting one is `denial`.  It does not delete the claim: it marks it
# suppressed, which stops the contradiction costing tension -- and a suppressed
# memory goes on ruminating at full strength anyway.  The mind drops the fact and
# the body keeps the feeling.  Watch Alice's fear stay exactly where it was while
# her confidence in what happened collapses.
#
# `acceptance` finally collapses the stances into one, and charges grief for it.

alice = agent

fear alice 0.6
remember alice "the door was open"
grief alice "the door was open" 0.5

recall alice "the door was open"

# She decides it did not happen.
denial alice "the door was open"
tick 3
recall alice "the door was open"
observe alice

# And then is not sure that it did not happen.
doubt alice "the door was open" 0.6
hope alice "the door was open" 0.4
tick 3
recall alice "the door was open"

# Living with it costs something. So does putting it down.
acceptance alice "the door was open"
tick 2
recall alice "the door was open"
observe alice
